# The `radio-wifi` producer: the Wi-Fi userland facts, as two disjoint packages.
#
# Plain `KEY=value` lines only: no logic, no command substitution, no
# expansion. Safe to source from bash and to parse with grep/sed from anything
# else, and checked for that shape by build-env/deb/build.sh before it is
# sourced. build-env/deb/README.md documents every field.

# Two packages, one producer. They are separate because a board chooses Wi-Fi
# client and access point INDEPENDENTLY: a combined package would make a
# station-only board install hostapd. Bluetooth is its own producer
# (radio-bluetooth) for the same reason at the producer
# level -- selecting, declining and rebuilding one radio must not touch the
# other. No path and no relationship is shared between any of them.
PACKAGES="mica-wifi mica-wifi-ap"

# `all` and not `amd64 arm64`. Every payload here is a systemd unit or a
# symlink, with no ELF anywhere, so one archive is correct for every image this
# repository builds.
ARCHES="all"

# `family` is producers/radio, and it is here for one file: the copyright,
# carried over verbatim from mica-system with these producers. The primary
# build context is this producer's OWN directory, so anything held in common
# with the sibling producer sits in producers/radio and has to be named to be
# reachable.
#
# The unit bodies are copied BYTE-IDENTICAL out of the tree that ships them,
# so that packaging them cannot become a second, drifting rendering of the
# same files. They live under producers/radio, beside the Bluetooth ones;
# their content is a radio-application fact, not a board fact -- the board facts they are paired with (the radio kernel modules, the
# firmware) stay in mica-board-cx3576.
BUILD_CONTEXTS="family=producers/radio radio-units=producers/radio/units"

# mica-wifi enables ONE unit at multi-user.target -- mica-regdb-reload.service,
# the cfg80211 regulatory-database load that a built-in cfg80211 on a board
# with no initramfs cannot do for itself. Its etc-wpa_supplicant.mount ships a
# local-fs.target.wants link as well, which this field does not count.
# mica-wifi-ap enables nothing: its only link is the local-fs one for
# etc-hostapd.mount, so it writes the zero rather than leaving a reader to
# infer it from a payload that does contain enablement links.
ENABLEMENT="mica-wifi=1 mica-wifi-ap=0"
